===Etymology===
{{unc|pl}}.<ref>{{R:pl:WSEHJP}}</ref> {{displaced|pl|niewiasta}} (now considered poetic) and {{m|pl|żona}} (meaning narrowed down to "wife"). Popularized by Polish romanticist writers in the 19th century. {{etydate|1545|ref={{R:pl:SXVI|+|58143}}}}
* Possibly from {{der|pl|zlw-opl|kob|t=pigpen|nocat=1}} or {{m|zlw-opl|koba|t=mare}} (the suffix {{m|pl||-ieta}} is found in some contemporaneous female names ({{m|pl|Markieta}}, {{m|pl|Elżbieta}}, {{m|pl|Greta}})), however this theory has been rejected by most etymologists.<ref>{{R:pl:Bruckner}}</ref> Compare {{m|pl|kobiałka}}.
* Boryś asserts the term is a Polish innovation with an unclear root ''kob-'' and potentially the original suffix being suffixed with {{m|pl|-ita}} (compare {{cog|zlw-mpl|kobita}} and {{m|pl|najmita}}), saying that the suffix {{m|pl||-ita}} doesn't exist.<ref>{{R:pl:Boryś}}</ref>
* Bańkowski asserts that the term is not native, and asserts {{der|pl|gem|nocat=1}} origin, possibly a compound of {{der|pl|gmh|kobe|nocat=1||sty, shed}} (modern {{cog|de|Koben}} + {{m|gmh||*geit|t=goat}} (modern {{cog|nl|geit}}), originally meaning a pen for goats but also derogatorily for service maidens, however he also questions why the form is ''-bie-'' and not ''-be-''.<ref>{{R:pl:Bańkowski}}</ref>
* Sławski also refutes native origin, but ultimately unable to draw connections to other languages.<ref>{{R:pl:Sławski}}</ref>

